{
 "coreName": "Fuse",
 "systemId": "ZXS",
 "systemNames": {
  "ZXS": "ZX Spectrum"
 },
 "author": "Philip Kendall and the Fuse team; chimera port by Sergio Martin",
 "url": "https://github.com/ToolAssisted-run/chimera-core-fuse",
 "runsOn": [
  "windows-x86_64",
  "linux-x86_64",
  "macos-x86_64"
 ],
 "romFile": "rom",
 "deterministic": true,
 "memoryLayoutMiB": [
  64,
  4,
  8,
  4,
  128
 ],
 "_memoryLayoutMiB_note": "sbrk, sealed, invisible, plain, mmap. A Spectrum is small - the biggest machine here has 1 MiB of memory - and so are its tapes and discs; the rest is room for Fuse's own tables.",
 "video": {
  "_comment": "width and height are the biggest picture any of the machines makes; the size of the picture in front of you comes from the core every frame. A Spectrum's picture is 256x192 dots inside a border, 320x240 in all. The Timex machines and the Spectrum SE have a mode with twice the dots across, so their picture is kept at 640x480. Both are the shape of a 4:3 television. The frame rate is the machine's own and comes from the core: 50.08 frames a second for a 48K Spectrum (3,500,000 processor cycles a second, 69,888 a frame), 50.02 for a 128K one, 59.65 for the NTSC and Timex TS2068 machines.",
  "width": 640,
  "height": 480,
  "virtualWidth": 640,
  "virtualHeight": 480,
  "vsyncNumerator": 3500000,
  "vsyncDenominator": 69888,
  "getBgra": "GetVideoBgra"
 },
 "audio": {
  "_comment": "Fuse's mix of the beeper, the tape's sound and the 128K machines' sound chip, at 44100 samples a second in stereo: about 880 pairs a frame.",
  "rate": 44100,
  "samplesPerFrame": 4096,
  "channels": 2,
  "get": "GetAudio"
 },
 "lag": {
  "inputWasRead": "InputWasRead"
 },
 "extensions": {
  ".tap": "ZXS",
  ".tzx": "ZXS",
  ".pzx": "ZXS",
  ".z80": "ZXS",
  ".sna": "ZXS",
  ".szx": "ZXS",
  ".trd": "ZXS",
  ".scl": "ZXS",
  ".mdr": "ZXS",
  ".dck": "ZXS",
  ".mgt": "ZXS",
  ".udi": "ZXS"
 },
 "input": {
  "name": "ZX Spectrum Keyboard and Joysticks",
  "_comment": "The forty keys of a Spectrum, row by row as they are printed on it; the tape recorder's Play and Stop; the Reset button; and two joysticks of four directions and a fire button. What a joystick is plugged into is the settings Joystick 1 and Joystick 2. On a Kempston interface a joystick is its own device; a Sinclair or cursor joystick presses keys of the keyboard, as the real ones do.",
